The Origins and Historical Development of the Dewanganj–Bakshiganj Region
A review of history shows that the greater Mymensingh region was part of the ancient Pragjyotishpura Kingdom until around the 4th century. The capital of the Pragjyotishpura Kingdom was Pragjyotishpura, which is now the major city of Guwahati in Assam. Later, in the 4th century, the kingdom was renamed Kamarupa after Pragjyotishpura. Around 1140 AD, the Kamarupa Kingdom gradually began to break up into several smaller kingdoms.
Historical records indicate that the Dewanganj region was under the Kamarupa Kingdom during this period. The area was characterized by river erosion, natural disasters, and difficult char lands. Even today, the language and culture of the people of this region retain considerable Kamarupi influence, which further highlights its historical connection with the ancient Kamarupa region.
Garh Dalipa
Garh Dalipa, now known as Garh Jaripa, was the capital of an ancient and independent kingdom in medieval greater Mymensingh. When the Kamarupa Kingdom weakened during the 13th and 14th centuries, several small independent and semi-independent kingdoms emerged at the foothills of the Garo Hills.
During this period, a powerful king of the Koch dynasty, Raja Dalip Samanta, established an independent kingdom in this region. Between the 14th and 15th centuries, Raja Dalip Samanta constructed a fort to protect his kingdom from external attacks. The fort was named Garh Dalipa after the king. Its capital was located in present-day Garh Jaripa Union of Sreebardi Upazila in Sherpur District.
Because of its fertile agricultural land, the Kingdom of Garh Dalipa was heavily dependent on the Brahmaputra basin. Until the end of the 15th century, the Kamata Kingdom maintained a strong position across North Bengal, Assam, and the northern parts of greater Mymensingh, including the northern areas of Jamalpur, Sherpur, and Netrokona.
However, the entire Mymensingh region was not under a single kingdom. These areas were divided between different kingdoms by the Brahmaputra River. Various indigenous groups, including the Koch, Mech, and Hajong, ruled different parts of the Brahmaputra basin.
The northern part of Mymensingh, particularly the northern upazilas of Sherpur and Jamalpur, was directly influenced by Kamarupa–Kamata culture, while other parts of greater Mymensingh were under the Bengal Sultanate. Between 1492 and the beginning of the 16th century, the Kamarupa Kingdom fell under the control of the Bengal Sultans, and the northern part of greater Mymensingh subsequently came under Bengal rule. Thus, the ancient Kamarupa–Kamata political influence in this region gradually came to an end.
Patiladaha Pargana
During British rule, in 1787, Patiladaha Pargana covered the entire northern part of Jamalpur, including the present-day upazilas of Islampur, Dewanganj, and Bakshiganj. It also included Bhabaniganj, present-day Fulchhari and surrounding areas of Gaibandha District. Historically, therefore, this pargana extended from eastern Mymensingh to the region of Rangpur.
The entire Patiladaha Pargana and its adjoining areas were under the zamindari of the Pradyot Narayan Thakurs. Pradyot Narayan Thakur was a close relative of Rabindranath Tagore and was once a member of the Tagore family.
In the Dharmakura area of Patiladaha Pargana, corresponding to present-day Islampur, the Thakur zamindars patronized the traditional brass industry, as well as various other folk arts and crafts.
The principal administrative and economic centers of the pargana were Pradyutnagar, present-day Bahadurabad Ghat in Dewanganj, and Rajendraganj in Bakshiganj, where the Thakur family's kachari houses were located. The ruins of the kachari houses in Islampur (Dharmakura) and Bakshiganj (Rajendraganj), along with the railway station at Dewanganj (Pradyutnagar), still remind us of the old Patiladaha Pargana and its zamindari system.
Until 1820, the Dewanganj region was under Rangpur District. In 1821, the region was incorporated into Bogura District, and later, in 1866, Dewanganj was included in the Jamalpur subdivision of Mymensingh District. Dewanganj Thana was established in 1874, while the Zilla Bangla Sugar Mill was established in 1858.
Earthquakes and Natural Disasters
In 1885, a devastating earthquake caused extensive damage across the district. Another powerful earthquake struck in 1897. During this earthquake, sand and water reportedly burst violently from deep underground. Most houses, trees, and other structures in the district were severely damaged or destroyed. At that time, many rivers, canals, and wetlands, including the Mrigi, Someshwari, and Dashani rivers, were filled with sediment.
The Peasant Uprising of 1907
In 1907, an incident surrounding the Jamalpur Public Fair led to clashes between Hindu zamindars and peasants. As a result, the following day, rebellious peasants looted Dewanganj Bazaar. The peasants had also become increasingly rebellious because of the excessive rents and taxes imposed by the zamindars in the area.
Railway and Bahadurabad Ghat
In 1911, when the British constructed a railway line from Dhaka to Bahadurabad, the present Dewanganj Railway Station was named Pradyutnagar. It is said that the name Pradyutnagar attracted considerable attention among travelers. The name remained in use until 1959.
According to local tradition, the Thakur Bahadurs used to travel to this ghat from Calcutta by large boats known as bajras. Because of this, the ghat on the bank of the Jamuna River gradually became known as Bahadurabad.
In 1938, the government introduced the Bahadurabad Railway Ferry Ghat on this route to facilitate transportation between Dhaka and northern Bangladesh. The railway ferry service operated under the former Assam-Bengal Railway. On the eastern bank was Bahadurabad Ghat, while on the opposite bank was Tistamukh Ghat in Gaibandha District.
In 1990, due to river erosion and the reduction of navigability in the Jamuna River, the ferry terminal was relocated from Bahadurabad to Balasi Ghat in Gaibandha. At one time, large launches and steamers regularly docked at both of these ghats. Due to continuous river erosion, Bahadurabad Ghat has since shifted from its original location and lost much of its former glory.
Remarkably, there was once a prestigious train known as the Bahadurabad Express, which operated between Chattogram and Bahadurabad Ghat.
The Liberation War of 1971
During the Liberation War of 1971, several bloody battles took place between the Pakistan Army and the freedom fighters at Bahadurabad Ghat on the Jamuna River.
Pakistani forces and their collaborators, the Razakars, captured and executed several freedom fighters and civilians at Bahadurabad Ghat. Freedom fighter Abdul Barek was martyred in a mine explosion at this location. Student leader Anwarul Azim Chhana was brutally tortured at Bahadurabad Ghat and was pushed toward death. Freedom fighter Mainul Hossain Saifullah was brutally killed and his body was thrown into the Jamuna River at Bahadurabad.
Various structures around the former Pradyutnagar Railway Station, now Dewanganj Railway Station, still bear reminders of the horrors of the Liberation War of 1971.
Finally, in 1982, Bakshiganj Upazila was created by dividing Dewanganj Upazila.

